-- title:
--   The Drinfeld curve coordinate ring is a domain
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a natural number with $q \neq 1$ and let $k$ be an arbitrary field (in an arbitrary universe). Consider the polynomial ring `MvPolynomial (Fin 2) k` in two variables $x = X_0$, $y = X_1$ over $k$, and inside it the ideal [`DrinfeldCurve.drinfeldIdeal q k`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L19), defined as the principal ideal generated by [`DrinfeldCurve.drinfeldPoly q k - 1`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L17), where the Drinfeld polynomial is $x y^q - x^q y$; the type [`DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing q k`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L21) is by definition the quotient of `MvPolynomial (Fin 2) k` by this ideal. The assertion is that this quotient ring is an integral domain, i.e. it is nontrivial and has no zero divisors. No restriction is placed on the characteristic of $k$, on $k$ being finite or perfect, or on $q$ beyond $q \neq 1$; in particular $q = 0$ and composite $q$ are allowed. The hypothesis $q \neq 1$ is needed, since for $q = 1$ the generator is the unit $-1$ and the quotient is the zero ring, which is not nontrivial.
--
--   This is the basic integrality statement for the affine curve $x y^q - x^q y = 1$, the Drinfeld curve, whose coordinate ring underlies the construction of its function field and Jacobian elsewhere in the development; it is cited by the computation of the Abel–Jacobi cardinality [`DrinfeldCurve.abelJacobiCard_drinfeldFunctionField`](thm.html#DrinfeldCurve.abelJacobiCard_drinfeldFunctionField) and by statements about inertia at the level of newforms.
